    "description": "DuroMax's XP28000iH is the largest inverter portable we could find on sale, rated for 22,000 running and 28,000 peak watts on gasoline and 20,000 running on propane. Because it is an inverter, the 999cc engine varies its speed with the load rather than holding 3,600 rpm, and that is where its advantages come from. DuroMax lists 70 dB at 25% load and 16 hours from the 12.7-gallon tank at that load, or 9 hours at 50%, which works out to roughly 0.8 gallons an hour at a typical overnight load. The panel has two 14-50R outlets, an L14-30R and four 5-20R receptacles. At 466 pounds it is lighter than the Westinghouse twins but still not something you lift. The risk is newness: it has little owner history so far, so long-term reliability is unknown. If you want 20kW-class output and care about noise or overnight fuel, this is the pick."
  },
  {
    "id": "westinghouse-wgen20000tfc",
    "name": "Westinghouse WGen20000TFc",
    "category": "Tri-Fuel Open Frame, 20,000W Running / 28,000W Peak",
    "badge": "Best Tri-Fuel",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 380,
    "pros": [
      "Runs on gasoline, propane or natural gas: 20,000 / 18,000 / 16,000 running watts",
      "125A 240V terminal block in addition to two 14-50R and two L14-30R outlets",
      "Natural gas hookup means no refueling during a long outage",
      "Remote start and CO sensor shutoff"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "557 lb dry, the heaviest unit here",
      "Propane runtime from a 20 lb tank is rated at only 3 to 5 hours"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one to buy if you have a natural gas line and want a portable that behaves like a small standby. Plan the connection before you plan the purchase.",
    "description": "The WGen20000TFc takes the same 999cc platform as the WGen20000c and adds propane and natural gas. Westinghouse rates it at 20,000 running watts on gasoline, 18,000 on propane and 16,000 on natural gas, with peaks of 28,000, 25,200 and 22,400. The detail that matters most is the 125A 240V terminal block next to the two 14-50R and two L14-30R outlets, because it lets an electrician feed a properly sized transfer switch rather than squeezing 20kW through a 50-amp cord. On a natural gas line it can run as long as the oil holds out, which removes the main weakness of any gas portable. Propane is the weak fuel: Westinghouse lists just 3 hours at 25% load and 5 at 50% from a 20 lb cylinder, so plan on a 100 lb tank or larger. At 557 pounds dry it is the heaviest generator in this guide, and it will mostly live where it runs."
  },
  {
    "id": "generac-gp18000efi",
    "name": "Generac GP18000EFI 8917",
    "category": "Gas Open Frame with EFI, 18,000W Running / 22,500W Starting",
    "badge": "Best Brand Support",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "rating": 4.4,
    "reviewCount": 260,
    "pros": [
      "18,000 running watts from an 816cc fuel-injected engine with no carburetor to gum up",
      "CO-sense shutoff and 50-state compliance on the 8917 model",
      "Generac's dealer network for parts and service",
      "Rated for 9 hours at 50% load from a 14.2-gallon tank"
    ],
