        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 20,000-watt generator enough to run a whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most houses, yes, including central air, a well pump and an electric range at the same time. The catch is the connection: through a standard 50-amp inlet, only 12,000 watts reach the panel. To use the full output, you need a 100-amp inlet or transfer switch installed by an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much fuel does a 20,000-watt generator use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Based on manufacturer runtime ratings, roughly 1.2 gallons an hour at quarter load and about 2 gallons an hour at half load for an open-frame 20kW unit. The DuroMax XP28000iH inverter works out to about 0.8 gallons an hour at quarter load. Over a full day of running, plan on 25 to 50 gallons.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a 20,000-watt generator weigh?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Between about 440 and 560 pounds dry. Westinghouse lists the WGen20000c at 541 pounds and the tri-fuel WGen20000TFc at 557. The DuroMax XP28000iH is 466 and the Generac GP18000EFI is 442.8. A full tank adds close to 100 pounds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between 20,000 running and 20,000 peak watts?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Running watts are what the generator can carry continuously. Peak watts are a brief surge for starting motors. A 20,000-peak generator is often a 15,000 to 16,000-running generator. The Westinghouse WGen20000 models are rated at 20,000 running and 28,000 peak, so they are true 20kW units.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a 20,000-watt generator into my house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only through a transfer switch or a code-compliant interlock kit with an inlet box, installed by a licensed electrician. Never backfeed through a dryer outlet. For the full 20,000 watts, the connection must be rated for 100 amps or more, which usually means a terminal-block or hardwired hookup.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 20,000-watt portable cheaper than a standby generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The generator itself is usually cheaper. Once you add a 100-amp transfer switch, a gas connection and a permanent pad, the total installed cost often comes close to a 20 to 22kW air-cooled standby, which starts automatically and does not need refueling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the quietest 20,000-watt generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Of the units here, the DuroMax XP28000iH inverter has the lowest listed rating at 70 dB at 25% load. The Westinghouse open-frame models are listed at 74 dBA. All of them are louder than a typical home standby, so put them at least 20 feet from the house and away from bedroom windows.
              </p>
            </div>

